     1	# Forward versus inverse optimization: precise resolutions
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-10-14 (America/Los_Angeles).
     4	
     5	## 1. Scope and prior theorem
     6	
     7	The natural **full-linear, binary, known-feasible-start** version is already solved, not a new open problem. Andreas S. Schulz, *On the Relative Complexity of 15 Problems Related to 0/1-Integer Programming* (2009), DOI [10.1007/978-3-540-76796-1_19](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-76796-1_19), Assumptions 1–2 and Theorem 1, proves equivalence of optimization, optimality verification, and inverse optimization under the 1- and infinity-norms. Its author manuscript is `sources/schulz-2009.pdf`. The proof below reproduces the essential verification-to-augmentation and bit-scaling arguments (Lemmas 19 and 5), making their promises explicit and extending the zero-test implication to other positive-definite distances. No novelty is claimed for this equivalence.
     8	
     9	The separate feasibility-only counterexamples below deliberately drop full cost-vector freedom. They do **not** refute Schulz's theorem. We did not obtain a natural counterexample with hard optimization after a feasible solution is supplied, nor could one exist in the full-linear binary model unless P=NP.
    10	
    11	## 2. Encoded model
    12	
    13	An instance descriptor I is a finite binary string. A polynomial-time computable dimension n=n(I) is polynomially bounded in |I|. The feasible set F(I) is a subset of {0,1}^n; membership (I,x) is decidable in polynomial time. F depends on I, not on the objective vector. All integers are binary-encoded, and a rational is a signed numerator and positive denominator in binary. Input size counts all these bits, not numerical magnitudes.
    14	
    15	The **full-cost model** allows every c in Q^n. Forward optimization returns x in F minimizing c·x, or reports F empty. The **known-start version** additionally receives x_start in F; consequently its inputs are nonempty. The inverse instance receives (I,c,x0,w), where x0 in F and all distance weights w_i are strictly positive rationals. It must output an exact optimal modified cost d in Q^n. Ties are allowed: x0 must be an optimizer, not the unique optimizer. In the main model d is unrestricted. Its feasible region is the closed rational polyhedral cone
    16	
    17	    N(I,x0) = {d : d·(x-x0) >= 0 for every x in F(I)}.
    18	
    19	The distances considered are:
    20	
    21	- D_1(d,c)=sum_i w_i |d_i-c_i|;
    22	- D_inf(d,c)=max_i w_i |d_i-c_i|;
    23	- D_2(d,c)^2=sum_i w_i (d_i-c_i)^2 (report squared value, avoiding an unnecessary square-root encoding);
    24	- D_Hsum(d,c)=sum_i w_i 1[d_i != c_i];
    25	- D_Hmax(d,c)=max({w_i : d_i != c_i} union {0}).
    26	
    27	Sum-Hamming and bottleneck-Hamming are different problems. No forward-P-to-inverse-P claim for sum-Hamming is assumed. The word 'polynomial' means deterministic bit-polynomial time and exact output, not pseudopolynomial time, approximation, or an oracle call counted as a free implementation.
    28	
    29	For the norm cases, an optimum exists: N is nonempty (0 belongs to it), closed, and the objective has bounded sublevel sets. For Hamming, only finitely many changed-coordinate supports exist; among supports admitting any feasible d choose one of minimum weight. Feasible rational linear systems have rational solutions of polynomial encoding length here, because their coefficients have polynomial bit size and dimension is polynomial. For squared weighted Euclidean distance, the unique projection is rational: restrict to the minimal face containing the projection and solve its rational equality-constrained quadratic program. A basis of active equations suffices, and determinant bounds give polynomial bit length. Thus the exact formulations above have finite encoded witnesses; they do not silently require a real-number oracle.
    30	
    31	## 3. Lemma Z: the inverse zero test is optimality verification
    32	
    33	For any listed distance, the exact optimum is zero if and only if x0 minimizes c·x over F. If x0 is optimal, d=c is feasible and has distance zero. Conversely, distance zero implies d=c since every weight is positive; inverse feasibility then says that x0 is optimal under c. This also works if the inverse algorithm returns only its exact minimizing d: test d=c. If the inverse problem imposes additional allowed-cost constraints, this lemma requires c itself to be allowed. It does not allow queries outside the advertised cost domain.
    34	
    35	An approximation oracle is not substituted for this exact zero test. For general norms, one cannot decide whether an approximate numerical zero is mathematically zero without further separation bounds.
    36	
    37	## 4. Lemma A: verification yields an improving feasible solution
    38	
    39	Assume an oracle V(I,q,x0) correctly answers whether the supplied feasible x0 minimizes q·x over F, for every rational q. Given integer c and x0 in F, the following algorithm either certifies optimality or constructs y in F with c·y<c·x0 using at most n+1 oracle calls.
    40	
    41	First call V(I,c,x0). If it says yes, stop. Otherwise set M=1+sum_i |c_i|, s_i=2x0_i-1, and S=empty. Process i=1,...,n. Query V at the vector
    42	
    43	    q_j = c_j - M s_j 1[j in S union {i}].
    44	
    45	If the answer is no, add i to S. If yes, leave S unchanged. Finally output y_j=x0_j for j in S and y_j=1-x0_j otherwise.
    46	
    47	**Proof.** For any T and any z in F,
    48	
    49	    q(T)·(z-x0) = c·(z-x0) + M |{j in T : z_j != x0_j}|.
    50	
    51	The absolute value of c·(z-x0) is at most sum_i |c_i|<M. Therefore z improves on x0 under q(T) if and only if z improves under the original c and agrees with x0 on every coordinate in T.
    52	
    53	Let B(S) be the set of original-c improving feasible points agreeing with x0 on S. Initially B(empty) is nonempty. A no answer means B(S union {i}) is nonempty, so the update preserves nonemptiness. A yes answer means every member of the current B(S) differs from x0 at coordinate i. Future B sets are subsets of this one, so this forced difference persists without needing to enforce it by an additional constraint. After all coordinates, B(S) is nonempty, and all its points agree with the reported y on every coordinate. Hence B(S)={y}, which proves feasibility and strict improvement. Costs in every query have bit length polynomial in the original input. No restricted feasible-set oracle, feasibility search, or self-reducibility hypothesis has been smuggled into the proof. QED.
    54	
    55	## 5. Theorem F: inverse in P implies forward in P from a feasible start
    56	
    57	For the model in Section 2, if any one of its five exact inverse variants is polynomial-time solvable, then the known-start forward problem is polynomial-time solvable.
    58	
    59	**Proof.** Lemma Z gives V; Lemma A gives augmentation. Scale rational c by the product of its positive denominators, producing an integer vector of polynomial encoding length with the same optimizers.
    60	
    61	For signed coefficients introduce virtual coordinates z_i=x_i when c_i>=0 and z_i=1-x_i when c_i<0. Then c·x equals a constant plus a·z, where a_i=|c_i|>=0. The virtual feasible set is in bijection with F. No new instance-class closure is needed: to query optimality for virtual cost p, query the original oracle with cost q_i=p_i on unflipped coordinates and q_i=-p_i on flipped coordinates, with the correspondingly mapped feasible target. Constants do not affect optimality.
    62	
    63	Let L be the maximum bit length of the a_i (L=0 if all are zero). Start with the supplied feasible z and p=0; z is optimal for p. Read the L binary positions of a from most significant to least significant. At each position let b in {0,1}^n be its bit vector and set p_new=2p+b. Repeatedly apply augmentation for p_new until the current z is optimal; then set p=p_new.
    64	
    65	At the start of a phase, z is optimal for p. For any z* in F minimizing p_new,
    66	
    67	    p_new·(z-z*) = 2p·(z-z*) + b·(z-z*) <= n.
    68	
    69	The first term is nonpositive, and each of the n terms in the second sum is at most 1. The gap is a nonnegative integer. Each successful augmentation decreases it by at least 1. Hence a phase uses at most n successful augmentations and one final optimality check. There are L phases. Lemma A uses at most n+1 verification queries per augmentation, so O(n^2 L) verification calls suffice, each of polynomial encoded size. The final virtual cost is a, and mapping back gives a forward optimum. The n=0 and c=0 cases return the supplied point immediately. QED.
    70	
    71	**Corollary F1.** If one can find a feasible point or correctly report emptiness in polynomial time, then inverse in P implies the ordinary forward problem in P. In particular, under P!=NP no full-cost binary family whose forward problem is NP-hard *even with a feasible start* can have any of these exact inverse variants in P.
    72	
    73	**Corollary F2 (constant cardinality).** Suppose every x in F has the same sum_i x_i. If the inverse zero test is available only for arbitrary nonnegative rational costs, the same conclusion holds: add a sufficiently large common constant to every coefficient of every signed-cost verification query. This makes the queried vector nonnegative while adding the same objective constant to all feasible points. The baseline vector must be admissible in the inverse model; finite upper bounds on all costs would require a separate argument.
    74	
    75	**Known reverse implication, with its limits.** For D_1 and D_inf, a forward polynomial algorithm separates the inequalities d·(x-x0)>=0 by minimizing d·x. Linearizing the distance with n absolute-value auxiliary variables or one radius variable yields a rational LP of polynomial dimension and polynomial-size coefficients. The polynomial-time separation-to-optimization theorem solves this LP, including any explicitly given rational cost bounds. This is the Ahuja–Orlin / Yang–Zhang direction stated as Theorem 3.3 in Heuberger. Together with F1 it gives equivalence for weighted l1 and l-infinity. It is not a proof of the same forward-to-inverse direction for sum-Hamming. Our complete elementary proof above is of the converse, which is the book's question under the stated hypotheses.
    76	
    77	## 6. Theorem G: a dummy-solution closure removes the feasible-start obstruction
    78	
    79	Assume a family can, in polynomial time, replace I by an instance J of the *same oracle-supported family* with feasible set, up to an explicitly known coordinate renaming,
    80	
    81	    F(J) = {(1,0^n)} union {(0,x) : x in F(I)}.
    82	
    83	Assume arbitrary rational costs are permitted on all n+1 coordinates. Then polynomial solvability of any inverse variant in Section 2 implies polynomial solvability of the ordinary forward problem, including detecting emptiness.
    84	
    85	**Proof.** Let B=1+sum_i |c_i|. Apply Theorem F to J with known starting point (1,0^n) and cost (B,c). Every real feasible point (0,x) has cost c·x<=sum_i |c_i|<B. Thus the returned optimum is the dummy exactly when F(I) is empty; otherwise its last n coordinates form an optimum for I. Everything has polynomial size. QED.
    86	
    87	A related threshold reduction uses cost (K+1/2,c) for integral c and integral K: the dummy is optimal if and only if there is **no** x in F(I) with c·x<=K. This turns an inverse zero test into the complement of the forward threshold test. If a problem is also polynomially self-reducible by prefix fixing, these threshold answers recover a witness one bit at a time, after an initial existence check. The stronger known-start proof above does not require prefix closure. Crucially, neither augmentation by a dummy solution nor prefix closure may be presumed for an arbitrary problem class.
    88	
    89	## 7. Proposition C: an explicit restricted-cost, feasibility-only counterexample
    90	
    91	This is a counterexample to the **broad reading allowing restricted cost parameters and forward infeasibility detection**, not to the full-cost model in Section 2.
    92	
    93	Let phi be a CNF formula on m variables and k>=1 be given in unary (or take k=1). Define
    94	
    95	    F(phi,k) = {(a,z) : a in {0,1}^m satisfies phi, z in {0,1}^k}.
    96	
    97	Feasibility checking is polynomial. Allow **only** cost vectors (0^m,b), b in Q^k, both as baseline and as modified costs. The objective is b·z. The forward task is to return an optimum or report F empty. It is NP-hard: phi is satisfiable if and only if the instance with b=0 has any feasible solution (equivalently, a feasible solution of value <=0). Thus a polynomial forward algorithm would decide SAT, contradicting P!=NP.
    98	
    99	For a valid inverse target (a0,z0), ignore the supplied satisfying assignment a0 after verifying it. For each free coordinate j, set
   100	
   101	    b'_j = max(b_j,0), if z0_j=0;
   102	    b'_j = min(b_j,0), if z0_j=1.
   103	
   104	This is an exact inverse optimum under every distance in Section 2, with weights on the k mutable coordinates. The fixed witness coordinates are zero in both vectors and contribute zero.
   105	
   106	**Proof of inverse optimality.** Since phi is satisfiable (a0 is supplied), every z is feasible paired with a0. Thus z0 is optimal precisely when b'_j>=0 for z0_j=0 and b'_j<=0 for z0_j=1, coordinate by coordinate. The displayed clipping is the closest point in each allowed half-line. Every sign-violating coordinate is forced to change, by an absolute amount at least |b_j|; all other coordinates can remain unchanged. These componentwise lower bounds imply optimality for weighted l1, squared weighted l2 and weighted l-infinity. The forced changed-coordinate set gives simultaneous lower bounds for both sum-Hamming and bottleneck-Hamming, achieved by clipping. Runtime is O(m+|phi|+k) arithmetic/comparison operations, with polynomial bit complexity. QED.
   107	
   108	**Degeneracy disclosure.** Once any feasible target is supplied, forward optimization is trivial: reuse its satisfying assignment and choose each z_j according to the sign of b_j. All forward hardness comes from locating any feasible witness / detecting emptiness, and arbitrary costs on the witness coordinates are prohibited. Enabling those costs destroys this inverse algorithm; for example, phi=(a1 OR a2), c=(1,1,0), and target (1,1,0) is not optimal, whereas the restricted algorithm would leave it unchanged.
   109	
   110	**Natural but equally degenerate example.** The unweighted Hamiltonian-cycle problem can be regarded as minimizing t times the number of chosen edges, where the only parameter is a single rational t. Every Hamiltonian cycle has exactly |V| edges. Given a cycle, the inverse optimum is always zero for any positive-definite distance on t, whereas producing a cycle or reporting none is NP-hard. This is a familiar combinatorial family but it has the same two defects: restricted uniform edge costs and feasibility-only hardness. It is not the weighted traveling-salesman inverse problem. No claim of a natural nondegenerate counterexample is made.
   111	
   112	## 8. Proposition L: exact unconstrained weighted l-infinity formula
   113	
   114	For any nonempty finite binary F and supplied x0, let s_i=2x0_i-1 and
   115	
   116	    r = max( {0} union {
   117	          c·(x0-x) / sum_{i:x_i!=x0_i}(1/w_i)
   118	          : x in F, x!=x0 } ).
   119	
   120	Then d_i=c_i-r*s_i/w_i is an optimal inverse cost vector and the minimum weighted l-infinity distance is r. A singleton F gives r=0.
   121	
   122	**Proof.** If D_inf(d,c)<=t, then
   123	
   124	    d·(x0-x) >= c·(x0-x) - t sum_{i:x_i!=x0_i}(1/w_i).
   125	
   126	Optimality of x0 requires the left side to be nonpositive, so t must be at least every positive ratio displayed above. Conversely, the proposed d gives equality in this bound simultaneously for every x: signs of x0_i-x_i are fixed by x0 wherever nonzero. The definition of r then gives d·(x0-x)<=0 for all x. Its distance is r (or zero for r=0). QED.
   127	
   128	The formula is a min-max characterization, **not a polynomial algorithm by enumeration**: F may be exponentially large. It supplies an exact independent mathematical reference for the small-instance checks, and connects to the min-max analysis in Bérczi–Mendoza-Cadena–Varga, arXiv:2302.13411, DOI 10.1007/s11590-024-02183-0.
   129	
   130	## 9. What is and is not settled
   131	
   132	1. Full-linear binary problems with arbitrary signed costs and a known/easily found feasible start: **settled affirmatively in prior literature (Schulz 2009)**. The preceding proof is a reproduced argument with executable exact checks. Hamming inverse-P implies forward-P follows by the same zero-test implication; no equivalence for sum-Hamming is asserted.
   133	2. Broad models allowing restricted costs and feasibility-bearing forward tasks: **the literal universal implication is false under P!=NP**, by C. The construction is intentionally degenerate and is not advertised as solving the intended nondegenerate question.
   134	3. Full-cost binary families with arbitrary implicit feasibility, no supplied/easily found initial point, and no dummy closure: **not resolved here**. Our algorithms need their first feasible target. The restricted-cost examples do not provide a counterexample to this exact remaining statement. We make no claim that the literature has proved this most general remaining formulation open; our search did not settle it.
   135	4. General nonlinear objectives, changing constraints/edge lengths, arbitrary real feasible sets, partial target observations, restricted perturbation domains, or KKT points in place of global optima require separate theorems. Heuberger's broad wording does not specify all the hypotheses needed to transfer the binary equivalence. The July 2025 Springer chapter still calls the broad NP-hard-forward/P-inverse question unresolved in its public abstract. This is evidence about that publication, not a contradiction to Schulz's explicitly conditional theorem.
   136	
   137	## 10. Verification boundary
   138	
   139	The complete arguments above, rather than finite experiments, establish the universally quantified claims. `work/check.py` exercises both the clipping inverse algorithm and the verification-to-forward reduction against independent exhaustive enumerators on finite test sets; its output is saved under `evidence/check-results.json`. `work/algorithms.py` exposes the actual reduction and receives only a yes/no optimality callback, never a hidden optimizing callback. The independent model review and resolution record are under `evidence/`. These are mathematical proofs with executable sanity checks, not Lean/kernel-checked formalizations.

