     9	
    10	Summary. An integral part of combinatorial optimization and computational com-
    11	plexity consists of establishing relationships between different problems or different
    12	versions of the same problem. In this chapter, we bring together known and new, pre-
    13	viously published and unpublished results, which establish that 15 problems related
    14	to optimizing a linear function over a 0/1-polytope are polynomial-time equivalent.
    15	This list of problems includes optimization and augmentation, testing optimality and
    16	primal separation, sensitivity analysis and inverse optimization, and several others.
    17	
    18	
    19	
    20	1 Introduction
    21	The equivalence of optimization and separation has been one of the most
    22	consequential results in combinatorial optimization, and beyond. For exam-
    23	ple, it gave rise to the first polynomial-time algorithms for finding maximum
    24	stable sets in perfect graphs, and for minimizing submodular functions, to
    25	mention just two of several results of this kind derived by Grötschel, Lovász,
    26	and Schrijver [9]. This equivalence has also paved the way for establishing
    27	negative results. For instance, it was instrumental in showing that computing
    28	the weighted fractional chromatic number is NP-hard [9]. For general lin-
    29	ear optimization problems and corresponding polyhedra, the equivalence be-
    30	tween optimization and separation holds under certain technical assumptions.
    31	For linear combinatorial optimization problems and associated 0/1-polytopes,
    32	however, these requirements are naturally satisfied. In fact, for such problems
    33	this equivalence not only means that a polynomial-time algorithm for one of
    34	the two problems implies that the other problem can be solved in polynomial
    35	time as well; if one of the two problems can be solved in strongly polynomial
    36	time, then so can the other [7, 10].
    37	    The relative computational complexity of solving one problem versus an-
    38	other has been studied in other situations as well. For example, Papadimitriou
    39	and Steiglitz wrote in their 1982 textbook on combinatorial optimization that
    40	“An Optimization Problem is Three Problems” [20, Page 343]. The other two
    41	2      Andreas S. Schulz
    42	
    43	problems they referred to, in addition to finding an optimal solution, are com-
    44	puting the cost of an optimal solution—called the evaluation problem—and,
    45	in case of minimization problems, deciding whether there exists a feasible
    46	solution of cost at most a given value. The latter problem is known as the
    47	recognition or decision problem. It is obvious that the optimization problem
    48	is at least as hard as the evaluation problem, which, in turn, is no easier than
    49	the decision problem. Papadimitriou and Steiglitz went on by asking:
    50	    Is it the case that all these versions are roughly of the same complex-
    51	    ity? In other words, can we solve the evaluation version by making
    52	    efficient use of a hypothetical algorithm that solves the recognition
    53	    version, and can we do the same with the optimization and evaluation
    54	    versions, respectively?                                    [20, page 345]
    55	Answers to these questions are known: Binary search over the range of pos-
    56	sible values reduces the evaluation problem to solving a polynomial number
    57	of decision problems. The reduction from the optimization problem to the
    58	evaluation problem is oftentimes illustrated with the help of the Traveling
    59	Salesman Problem: Consider the arcs of the given directed graph in order,
    60	and for each arc solve the evaluation problem for the same instance where the
    61	cost of the current arc is increased by one. If the cost of an optimal tour is the
    62	same as before, then there is an optimal tour that does not use the current arc.
    63	The cost of this arc remains incremented, and the algorithm proceeds with
    64	the next arc. If, however, the cost of an optimal tour is higher than before,
    65	then the current arc is part of an optimal tour. Its cost is changed back to the
    66	original value, and the algorithm continues.
    67	    In this chapter, we prove that 15 problems, including augmentation, deci-
    68	sion, evaluation, inverse optimization, optimization, primal separation, sensi-
    69	tivity analysis, separation, and testing optimality, are equivalent in Grötschel,
    70	Lovász, and Schrijver’s and Papadimitriou and Steiglitz’s sense: Given a hy-
    71	pothetical (strongly) polynomial-time algorithm for any one of them, each of
    72	the other problems can be solved in (strongly) polynomial time as well.
    73	    The chapter is organized as follows. In Section 2, we introduce the setup,
    74	in particular the class of linear combinatorial optimization problems consid-
    75	ered here, and necessary background information, such as oracle-polynomial
    76	time algorithms. Section 3 constitutes the main part of this chapter. In Sec-
    77	tion 3.1, we present the 15 problems in detail and state the main result.
    78	Section 3.2 is reserved for its proof, which is broken down into several sepa-
    79	rate results pertaining to solving one problem with the help of a hypothetical
    80	algorithm for another problem. This part is followed by a collection of notes
    81	and references, which attribute the individual results to their respective au-
    82	thors (Section 3.3). Consequences and implications of the equivalence of the
    83	15 problems are discussed in Section 4, including a proof of the Hirsch conjec-
    84	ture for 0/1-polytopes, a simplex-type algorithm for linear programming over
    85	0/1-polytopes that visits only polynomially many vertices, and complexity
    86	results for exact local search. Finally, Sections 5 and 6 discuss extensions of
    87	                          On the Complexity of 0/1-Integer Programming            3
    88	
    89	some of the results to local search and general integer linear programming,
    90	respectively.
    91	
    92	
    93	2 Preliminaries
    94	This chapter is concerned with linear combinatorial optimization problems. A
    95	linear combinatorial optimization problem Π consists of a family of instances
    96	(N, F, c), where N = {1, 2, . . . , n} is the ground set, F ⊆ P  2N is the set of
    97	                                            n
    98	feasible solutions, and the vector c ∈ Z assigns a cost of i∈F ci to every
    99	feasible solution F ∈ F.1 The objective is to find a feasible solution of minimal
   100	cost.2 The set of feasible solutions of a combinatorial optimization problem is
   101	usually not described explicitly, but is given implicitly, such as the family of
   102	all stable sets in an undirected graph, or the set of all Hamiltonian tours in
   103	a directed graph. Typically, if we have one instance of such a combinatorial
   104	optimization problem, then the same instance in which only the objective
   105	function coefficients are changed, is also an instance of the same combinatorial
   106	optimization problem. We make this our first assumption.
   107	Assumption 1. Let Π be a linear combinatorial optimization problem. If
   108	(N, F, c) describes an instance of Π, and d ∈ Zn , then (N, F, d) is an in-
   109	stance of Π as well.
   110	    Our interpretation of this assumption is that certain computational prop-
   111	erties of the problems considered here, such as polynomial-time solvability,
   112	depend only on the structure of F, but not on that of c.3 As a consequence of
   113	Assumption 1, we henceforth use Π to just denote the family of pairs (N, F),
   114	knowing that each such pair together with any compatible objective function
   115	vector constitutes an instance of the associated optimization problem. This
   116	convention allows us to refer to Π, even if we consider algorithmic problems
   117	other than optimization, which are defined over the instances (N, F) of Π.
   118	    It is well known that the optimization problem associated with Π can be
   119	stated equivalently as a 0/1-integer programming problem. Namely,
   120	             nX             o                            
   121	         min      ci : F ∈ F = min cx : x ∈ X = min cx : x ∈ P ,
   122	              i∈F
   123	                                           
   124	where, for a given instance (N, F, c), X := χF : F ∈ F is the set of incidence
   125	vectors of all feasible solutions, P := conv {X} is its convex hull, and cx
   126	1
   127	  The reason that these problems are called linear, or sometimes min-sum combina-
   128	  torial optimization problems is that the cost functions are linear over the ground
   129	  set.
   130	2
   131	  For the sake of definiteness, we consider minimization problems only. All results
   132	  stated in this chapter apply equally to maximization problems.
   133	3
   134	  The most relevant exception to this premise are nonnegative cost coefficients, and
   135	  we will comment later on which results of Section 3 remain valid if we restrict
   136	  ourselves to such instances.
   137	4       Andreas S. Schulz
   138	                                                        Pn
   139	denotes the inner product of c and x, i.e., cx =           i=1 ci xi . The incidence
   140	vector χF of a feasible solution F ∈ F is defined as χF      i := 1 if i ∈ F , and
   141	χFi  := 0,  otherwise. Thus,   X  offers an equivalent  representation    of feasible
   142	solutions as 0/1-points in the n-dimensional Euclidean space. Moreover, P is
   143	a 0/1-polytope, i.e., a polytope whose vertices have coordinates 0 or 1 only.
   144	Hereafter, we will use X to refer to the set of feasible solutions, as it will be
   145	convenient to work with 0/1-vectors instead of sets.
   146	    We assume that the reader is familiar with the concepts of polynomial-time
   147	and strongly polynomial-time algorithms, and we refer to [12] for more infor-
   148	mation on these and other notions of relevance to combinatorial optimization,
   149	which are not introduced here. One concept that is of particular importance
   150	to this chapter is that of an oracle-polynomial time algorithm, which helps us
   151	formalize the abstraction of a “hypothetical algorithm.” An oracle-polynomial
   152	time algorithm is a polynomial-time algorithm that, in addition to all standard
   153	operations, may also make a polynomial number of calls to a given oracle. In
   154	particular, the time it would take to compute the answers that the oracle pro-
   155	vides is not counted. However, if there were a polynomial-time algorithm for
   156	finding these answers, then one could replace the oracle by this algorithm, and
   157	the oracle-polynomial time algorithm would turn into a regular polynomial-
   158	time algorithm. The definition of a strongly oracle-polynomial time algorithm
   159	is similar. For technical details, we refer to [10, Chapter 1].
   160	    The concept of oracles and oracle-polynomial time algorithms is useful to
   161	relate the complexity of two different problems, defined over the same family
   162	Π of sets of feasible solutions, to one another. For instance, if there is an
   163	oracle-polynomial time algorithm for optimization that uses an oracle for the
   164	separation problem, then the optimization problem for Π is not harder than
   165	the separation problem for Π, at least as far as polynomial-time solvability is
   166	concerned. The concept of oracles also provides us with means to specify the
   167	way in which sets of feasible solutions are given computationally. For example,
   168	when we say that X is given by an evaluation oracle, an oracle-polynomial
   169	time algorithm for the optimization problem has no further knowledge of X
   170	than what it can acquire by calling the evaluation oracle a polynomial number
   171	of times.
   172	    We make one further assumption, which frees us from considering how to
   173	find an initial feasible solution, which often is a difficult problem in itself.
   174	
   175	Assumption 2. Let Π be a linear combinatorial optimization problem. For
   176	any instance (N, X) of Π, a feasible solution x0 ∈ X is explicitly known.
   177	
   178	    Let us fix some additional notation. We use ei to denote the i-th unit
   179	vector, which has a 1 in coordinate i and 0 elsewhere. Moreover, 1l is the all-
   180	one vector. Finally, the support of a vector z ∈ Zn is defined by supp(z) :=
   181	{i ∈ N : zi 6= 0}.
