   184	3 An Optimization Problem is 15 Problems
   185	We start by detailing the computational problems defined over the instances
   186	(N, X) of a linear combinatorial optimization problem Π, which we will relate
   187	to one another.
   188	
   189	3.1 List of Problems
   190	
   191	Augmentation Problem (aug). Given a feasible solution x ∈ X and a vector
   192	c ∈ Zn , find a feasible solution y ∈ X such that cy < cx, or state that x
   193	minimizes cx over X.
   194	Component Determination Problem (cd). Given an index i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , n}
   195	and a vector c ∈ Zn , determine whether there exists an optimal solution x∗
   196	for minimizing cx over X such that x∗i = 1.
   197	Component Negation Problem (cn). Given an index i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , n}, a vector
   198	c ∈ Zn , and an optimal solution x∗ for minimizing cx over X, find an optimal
   199	solution for minimizing cx over {x ∈ X : xi = 1 − x∗i }, if one exists.
   200	Decision Problem (dec). Given a vector c ∈ Zn and a number K ∈ Z, decide
   201	whether X contains a feasible solution x with objective function value cx ≤ K.
   202	Evaluation Problem (eva). Given a vector c ∈ Zn , find the cost of a solution
   203	minimizing cx over X.
   204	Inverse Optimization Problem (inv). Given a feasible solution x ∈ X and a
   205	vector c ∈ Qn , find a vector d ∈ Qn such that x is optimal for minimizing dx
   206	over X and kc − dk is minimal.4
   207	Maximum Mean Augmentation Problem (mma). Given a feasible solution x ∈
   208	X and a vector c ∈ Zn , find a feasible solution y ∈ X such that cy < cx and
   209	y maximizes c(x − y)/|supp(x − y)| over X, or state that x minimizes cx over
   210	X.
   211	Optimization Problem (opt). Given a vector c ∈ Zn , find a feasible solution
   212	that minimizes cx over X.
   213	Postoptimality Problem (post). Given an index i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , n}, a vector
   214	c ∈ Zn , and an optimal solution x∗ for minimizing cx over X, compute the
   215	maximal value ρi ∈ Z+ such that x∗ remains optimal for minimizing (c+ρi ei )x
   216	and (c − ρi ei )x over X.
   217	Primal Separation Problem (psep). Given a point z ∈ [0, 1]n and a feasible
   218	solution y ∈ X, find a vector a ∈ Zn and a number β ∈ Z such that ax ≤ β
   219	for all x ∈ X, ay = β, and az > β, or state that such a vector a and number
   220	β do not exist.
   221	4
   222	    Instead of repeating the problem statement for different norms, let us merely
   223	    remark that one can use the 1-norm or the ∞-norm. They would generally lead to
   224	    different answers, of course. In terms of their computational complexity, however,
   225	    they behave the same.
   226	6      Andreas S. Schulz
   227	
   228	Separation Problem (sep). Given a point z ∈ [0, 1]n , decide whether z ∈ P =
   229	conv{X}, and if not, find a vector a ∈ Zn such that ax < az for all x ∈ P .
   230	Simultaneous Postoptimality Problem (spost). Given an index set I ⊆ {1, 2,
   231	. . . , n}, a vector c ∈ Zn , and an optimal solution x∗ for minimizing cx over
   232	X, compute the P     maximal value ρI ∈ Z+ such that x∗ remains optimal for
   233	minimizing (c + i∈I δi ei )x over X, for all (δi )i∈I with |δi | ≤ ρI .
   234	Unit Increment Problem (inc). Given an index i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , n}, a vector
   235	c ∈ Zn , and an optimal solution x∗ for minimizing cx over X, compute optimal
   236	solutions for the problems of minimizing (c − ei )x and (c + ei )x over X,
   237	respectively.
   238	Verification Problem (ver). Given a feasible solution x ∈ X and a vector
   239	c ∈ Zn , decide whether x minimizes cx over X.
   240	Violation Problem (viol). Given a vector a ∈ Zn and a number β ∈ Z,
   241	decide whether ax ≥ β holds for all x ∈ X, and if not, find a feasible solution
   242	y ∈ P = conv{X} with ay < β.
   243	    There are some obvious relationships between these problems. For in-
   244	stance, if we can solve the optimization problem in polynomial time, then
   245	we can also solve the augmentation problem and the evaluation problem in
   246	polynomial time. Moreover, a polynomial-time algorithm for evaluation gives
   247	rise to polynomial-time algorithms for the decision problem and the verifica-
   248	tion problem. And the polynomial-time solvability of augmentation or inverse
   249	optimization obviously implies the same for verification. Figure 1 shows all
   250	trivial relationships. Here, an arrow from one problem to another indicates
   251	that the problem at the head of the arrow can be solved in oracle-polynomial
   252	time given an oracle for the problem at the tail of the arrow. The relation is
   253	transitive, but arrows implied by transitivity are omitted.
   254	
   255	
   256	
   257	            SPOST          MMA       INV                     SEP
   258	
   259	
   260	             POST          AUG       VER                    PSEP
   261	
   262	
   263	                                     EVA                     CD
   264	
   265	             INC           OPT                  DEC
   266	                                                             CN
   267	                                    VIOL
   268	
   269	             Fig. 1. Obvious relationships between the 15 problems
   270	                           On the Complexity of 0/1-Integer Programming           7
   271	
   272	   It turns out that, in fact, all these problems are equivalent, in terms of
   273	polynomial-time solvability.
   274	
   275	Theorem 1. Let Π be a linear combinatorial optimization problem. Any one
   276	of the following problems defined over the instances (N, X) of Π, augmen-
   277	tation, component determination, component negation, decision, evaluation,
   278	inverse optimization, maximum mean augmentation, optimization, postopti-
   279	mality, primal separation, separation, simultaneous postoptimality, unit in-
   280	crement, verification, and violation, can be solved in oracle-polynomial time,
   281	given an oracle for any one of the other problems.
   282	
   283	    This list is by no means exhaustive. While it contains some of the most
   284	interesting and relevant computational questions related to combinatorial op-
   285	timization problems, other problems and variations of problems considered
   286	here can often proved to be equivalent by techniques similar to those illus-
   287	trated in the following section.
   288	
   289	3.2 Proof of Theorem 1
   290	
   291	Instead of presenting a “minimal” proof of Theorem 1, it will be instructive to
   292	exhibit several direct reductions between different problems. In the end, there
   293	will be a “directed path,” in the sense of Figure 1, from any one problem to
   294	any other problem, proving Theorem 1.
   295	    We first introduce some additional notation. For a given set X ⊆ {0, 1}n
   296	of feasible solutions and an index i ∈ {1, 2, . . . , n}, we define
   297	
   298	           X i,0 := {x ∈ X : xi = 0} and X i,1 := {x ∈ X : xi = 1}.
   299	
   300	Note that either X i,0 or X i,1 could be empty. For a given vector c ∈ Zn , and
   301	l ∈ {0, 1}, let xi,l be an optimal solution for min{cx : x ∈ X i,l }, if one exists.
   302	The following observation will prove useful on several occasions.
   303	
   304	Observation 2. Let x∗ be an optimal solution for minimizing cx over X.
   305	(a) If x∗i = 1, then (c + δei )x∗ ≤ (c + δei )x for all x ∈ X i,1 and δ ∈ Z.
   306	(b) If x∗i = 0, then (c + δei )x∗ ≤ (c + δei )x for all x ∈ X i,0 and δ ∈ Z.
   307	
   308	   We start the proof of Theorem 1 by looking at sets of feasible solutions
   309	specified by an augmentation oracle. In addition to the trivial relationship
   310	depicted in Figure 1, we have the following three lemmata.
   311	
   312	Lemma 3. Assume that X ⊆ {0, 1}n is given by an augmentation oracle.
   313	Then, the unit increment problem for X can be solved in oracle-polynomial
   314	time.
   315	
   316	Proof. Let the input to the unit increment problem be specified by x∗ , c,
   317	and i. Let us first consider the case x∗i = 0. Clearly, x∗ stays optimal for
   318	8       Andreas S. Schulz
   319	
   320	c + ei . Hence, it suffices to focus on the objective function vector c − ei . If x∗
   321	is no longer optimal for this vector, then xi,1 is. As (c − ei )x∗ − (c − ei )xi,1 =
   322	cx∗ − cxi,1 + 1 ≤ 1, one call to the augmentation oracle with input x∗ and
   323	c − ei will suffice to obtain an optimal solution for c − ei . The case x∗i = 1 can
   324	be handled similarly.                                                              t
   325	                                                                                   u
   326	
   327	Lemma 4. Assume that X ⊆ {0, 1}n is given by an augmentation oracle.
   328	Then, there exists an oracle-polynomial time algorithm for the inverse opti-
   329	mization problem over X.
   330	
   331	Proof. Suppose we would like to solve the inverse optimization problem for
   332	x and c, and we are given access to an augmentation oracle. A first call to
   333	the oracle with input x and c will clarify whether x is optimal with respect
   334	to c. If it is, then we return c itself. Otherwise, we can formulate the inverse
   335	optimization problem for the 1-norm as the following linear program:
   336	                            n
   337	                            X
   338	             minimize             λi                                           (1a)
   339	                            i=1
   340	            subject to λi ≥ di − ci                 for i = 1, 2, . . . , n,   (1b)
   341	                         λi ≥ ci − di               for i = 1, 2, . . . , n,   (1c)
   342	                         dx ≤ dy                          for all y ∈ X.       (1d)
   343	
   344	Given a vector (d, λ) ∈ Qn × Qn , the separation problem for the polyhedron
   345	defined by (1b)–(1d) can be solved as follows. Inequalities (1b) and (1c) can be
   346	checked directly. If one of them is violated, we obtain a separating hyperplane.
   347	If all of them are satisfied, we can call the augmentation oracle for x and d
   348	to find a violated inequality among (1d), if one exists. By the equivalence of
   349	separation and optimization, it follows that an optimal solution to the linear
   350	program (1) can be computed in polynomial time.
   351	    If we are dealing with the ∞-norm instead of the 1-norm, the argument
   352	remains essentially the same; we just replace the n variables λi by a single
   353	variable λ, and the objective function by λ.                                   t
