   356	   The next proof presents a reduction from optimization to augmentation,
   357	which, in contrast to the previous proof, does not rely on the equivalence of
   358	optimization and separation.
   359	
   360	Lemma 5. Assume that X ⊆ {0, 1}n is given by an augmentation oracle.
   361	Then, the optimization problem for X can be solved in oracle-polynomial time.
   362	
   363	Proof. The proof is based on scaling. To simplify the exposition, we assume
   364	that the given objective function vector c has nonnegative entries. If this is
   365	not the case, we can switch xi to 1 − xi for all coordinates i with ci < 0. The
   366	resulting objective function vector is nonnegative. Moreover, the augmenta-
   367	tion oracle for the original set X of feasible solutions can be used to solve the
   368	augmentation problem for the new set of feasible solutions, after switching.
   369	                           On the Complexity of 0/1-Integer Programming             9
