    39	                                           1    Introduction
    40	                                           Inverse optimization problems have long been the focus of research due to their wide applicability
    41	                                           in both theory and practice. The roots of inverse optimization go back to the work of Burton and
    42	                                           Toit [6] who studied the inverse shortest paths problem, that is, the problem of recovering the
    43	                                           edge costs given some information about the shortest paths in the graph. Since their pioneering
    44	                                           work, countless of applications and extensions emerged; we refer the interested reader to [23]
    45	                                           for the basics and to [8, 14] for surveys.
    46	                                               In a classical optimization problem, we are given a set of feasible solutions together with a
    47	                                           linear cost function, and the goal is to find a feasible solution that minimizes or maximizes the
    48	                                           cost. In contrast, in an inverse optimization problem we are also given a fixed feasible solution,
    49	                                           and the goal is to modify the costs ‘as little as possible’ so that the input solution becomes
    50	                                           optimal. There are various ways to measure the deviation of the new cost function from the
    51	
    52	                                                                                              1
    53	original one, and, as one would expect, the choice of the objective greatly affects the complexity
    54	of the problem. In order to avoid confusion, we refer to the solutions of the inverse optimization
    55	problem and those of the underlying combinatorial optimization problem as feasible deviation
    56	vectors and solutions, respectively.
    57	     In the past decades, inverse optimization problems found numerous applications. As an
    58	example, let us briefly describe the Pathway concordance problem [7]. A clinical pathway de-
    59	scribes a standardized sequence of steps for managing a clinical process in the delivery of care
    60	for a specific disease, with the aim of optimizing the outcome on a patient or population-level.
    61	These processes are determined by multidisciplinary medical experts, and have been shown to
    62	efficiently improve e.g. patient survival and satisfaction, wait times, and cost of care. However,
    63	patients’ journeys through the healthcare system can differ significantly from the recommended
    64	pathways, which raises the problem of measuring the concordance of patient-traversed pathways
    65	against the recommended ones. The problem can be modeled by a directed graph whose vertices
    66	correspond to activities that the patient can undertake, and the arcs indicate that a patient went
    67	from one activity to another. The ‘cost’ of a patient undertaking or missing certain activities
    68	and traversing arcs can be modeled by arc costs. The goal is to determine arc costs such that
    69	the reference pathways are optimal, that is, they are shortest paths between the corresponding
    70	start and end vertices. Then, assuming such arc costs are available, the journey of any patient
    71	can be scored based on the cost of the associated directed walk through the network.
    72	     The present work is the first member of a series of papers. Our general goal is to give min-
    73	max characterizations and simple algorithms for inverse optimization problems under various
    74	objectives. Here we set up the basics of a general framework that uses a Newton-type approach
    75	for finding an optimal deviation vector, and derive algorithms for the weighted bottleneck
    76	Hamming distance and weighted ℓ∞ -norm objectives that follow the proposed scheme.
    77	     In the second part [5], we focus on a novel objective called the weighted span that aims at
    78	finding a ‘balanced’ or ‘fair’ deviation vector, and we propose an analogous algorithm for that
    79	objective. Nevertheless, the analysis of the algorithm there is much more involved due to the
    80	different nature of the span compared to the ℓ∞ -norm.
