    82	Previous work. Inverse optimization under the weighted bottleneck Hamming distance ob-
    83	jective1 has been of great interest recently. One of the earliest results is due to Duin and
    84	Volgenant [10] who considered the inverse minimum spanning tree, the inverse shortest path
    85	tree and the linear assignment problems. Liu and Yao [18] gave a strongly polynomial algorithm
    86	for the weighted inverse maximum perfect matching problem. An algorithm with an improved
    87	running time based on a binary search technique was later given by Tayyebi [24], and an analo-
    88	gous algorithm for the inverse matroid problem was given by Aman, Hassanpour and Tayyebi [3].
    89	Mohaghegh and Baroughi Bonab [20] showed that the inverse min-max spanning r-arborescence
    90	problem is solvable in strongly polynomial time. Guan, He, Pardalos and Zhang [13] presented
    91	a mathematical model for the inverse max+sum spanning tree problem, together with a method
    92	to check feasibility and a binary search algorithm for solving it. Karimi, Aman and Dolati [16]
    93	studied the inverse shortest s-t path problem and provided an LP-based algorithm which can
    94	be applied for some inverse multiobjective problem as well. Tayyebi and Aman [25] consid-
    95	ered a general inverse linear programming problem, and proposed an algorithm that is based
    96	on a binary search technique. As an application, they specialized the method for solving the
    97	corresponding inverse minimum-cost flow problem in strongly polynomial time. Nguyen and
    98	Hung [21] studied the so-called inverse connected p-median problem under the unweighted bot-
    99	tleneck Hamming distance objective. In this problem, the goal is to modify vertex weights of a
   100	block graph at minimum total cost so that a predetermined set of p connected vertices becomes
