   118	showed that the problem can be formulated as a maximum-mean alternating cycle problem in
   119	an undirected network, and can be solved in polynomial time by a binary search algorithm
   120	and in strongly polynomial time by an ascending algorithm. Using LP descriptions, Ahuja and
   121	Orlin [2] proved that if an optimization problem can be modeled as an LP, then the same holds
   122	for the underlying inverse optimization problem under ℓ1 - or ℓ∞ -norm objectives. Furthermore,
   123	if the optimization problem is polynomially solvable for linear cost functions, then the inverse
   124	counterparts with ℓ1 - and ℓ∞ -norms are also polynomially solvable. In [30], Zhang and Liu
   125	proposed a model that generalizes numerous inverse combinatorial optimization problems when
