  1241	At the end of this survey, we propose the following types of problems in the area of inverse
  1242	combinatorial optimization which—to the best of our knowledge—are still open:
  1243	REFERENCES ON INVERSE OPTIMIZATION                                                                 23
  1244	
  1245	
  1246	   1. Although most combinatorial problems that are polynomially solvable have polynomially
  1247	      solvable inverse problems (cf. Theorem 3.3), there are examples of polynomially solvable
  1248	      optimization problems that have N P-hard inverse problems (e. g. the inverse center location
  1249	      problem, see Section 4.18).
  1250	        Consider the following “inverse” question: Given an optimization problem (2.1) with a
  1251	        polynomially solvable inverse problem (2.2) (for all c and x̂). What does this imply for the
  1252	        complexity of the original problem (2.1)? Does it necessarily belong to P?
  1253	        Since the verification problem (given a c and a feasible x̂, prove that x̂ is optimal) can
  1254	        be answered by solving the inverse problem (x̂ is optimal if and only if c = ĉ), the original
  1255	        problem has to belong to co-N P. Hence it is unlikely that one can find an N P-hard problem
  1256	        with polynomial-time solvable inverse problem because this would imply co-N P = N P.
  1257	   2. For some common and strongly polynomial combinatorial optimization problems, the inverse
  1258	      problem can be proved to be solvable in strongly polynomial time, too. But there are some
  1259	      strongly polynomial combinatorial optimization problems, where no strongly polynomial
  1260	      time algorithm for the inverse problem is known. For instance, this is the case for the
  1261	      inverse maximum weight perfect matching problem under l1 norm in general graphs (cf.
  1262	      Section 4.16).

