    17	Abstract
    18	Partial inverse combinatorial optimization problems are bilevel optimization problems in
    19	which the leader aims to incentivize the follower to include respectively not include given
    20	sets of elements in the solution of their combinatorial problem. If the sets of required and
    21	forbidden elements define a complete follower solution and the follower problem is solvable
    22	in polynomial time, then the inverse combinatorial problem is also solvable in polynomial
    23	time. In contrast, partial inverse problems can be NP-complete when the follower problem
    24	is solvable in polynomial time. This applies e.g. to the partial inverse min cut problem. In
    25	this paper, we consider partial inverse combinatorial optimization problems in which weights
    26	can only be increased. Furthermore, we assume that the lower-level combinatorial problem
    27	can be solved as a linear program. In this setting, we show that the partial inverse shortest
    28	path problem on a directed acyclic graph is NP-complete. Moreover, the partial inverse
    29	assignment problem is NP-complete. Both results even hold if there is only one required
    30	arc or edge, respectively. For solving partial inverse combinatorial optimization problems
    31	with only weight increases, we present a novel branch-and-bound scheme that exploits the
    32	difference in complexity between complete inverse and partial inverse versions of a problem.
    33	For both primal heuristics and node relaxations, we use auxiliary problems that are basically
    34	complete inverse problems on similar instances. Branching is done on follower variables.
    35	We test our approach on partial inverse shortest path, assignment and min cut problems, and
    36	computationally compare it to an MPCC reformulation as well as a decomposition scheme.
    37	
