    58	1 Introduction
    59	
    60	A partial inverse combinatorial problem (PICP) is a bilevel optimization problem in which
    61	the leader aims to find minimal-weight modifications for the objective of a follower’s com-
    62	binatorial problem such that there is a lower-level optimal solution extending a given partial
    63	lower-level solution. In a partial solution of a combinatorial problem some elements are
    64	required to be included while others are forbidden. It is, thus, a bilevel optimization problem
    65	with bound constraints on lower-level variables on the upper level. PICPs occur e.g. in eco-
    66	nomics when a planner wants rational agents to behave in a prescribed way, see e.g. [1]. A
    67	PICP can also model parameter estimation problems, in which one aims to infer parameter
    68	values that explain a partially observed action, see e.g. [2].
    69	    If the given lower-level solution is a complete solution, we can solve the resulting complete
    70	inverse combinatorial problem (ICP) in polynomial time if the lower-level problem itself is
    71	polynomial-time solvable [3]. However, as soon as the target solution is only partial, the
    72	partial inverse combinatorial problem can be N P -hard even if the lower-level problem is
    73	an ‘easy’ combinatorial optimization problem solvable in polynomial time. For example, the
    74	partial inverse min cut problem (PIMCP) is N P -complete [4].
    75	    Throughout most of this paper, we assume that the lower-level problem admits an LP
    76	formulation and is therefore solvable in polynomial time. Under this assumption, once we
    77	know a lower-level feasible solution that extends the given partial solution and is optimal
    78	on the lower level in an optimal solution of the PICP, we can compute optimal weight
    79	modifications for the PICP efficiently by solving the corresponding complete ICP. We call a
    80	lower-level feasible solution that extends the given partial lower-level solution a lower-level
    81	completion. Hence, the main challenge in solving a PICP can be seen in determining which
    82	lower-level completion will be optimal for the follower in an optimal solution of the PICP.
    83	    In some cases, we can optimally solve a PICP in polynomial time by solving the corre-
    84	sponding complete ICP of a lower-level completion that is minimum-weight with respect
    85	to the initial weights. We call such a lower-level completion a minimum-weight lower-level
    86	completion, or just briefly minimal completion. Examples for PICPs that are solvable as com-
    87	plete ICPs for a minimal completion are the partial inverse shortest path problem (PISPP)
    88	on directed acyclic graphs (DAGs), see Theorem 2.11, and the partial inverse assignment
    89	problem (PIAP) [5], if arbitrary weight modifications are allowed.
