   855	4.1    Polynomially Solvable Cases
   856	
   857	The result of Ahuja and Orlin [2001] can be applied directly to observe that there are cases of the inverse
   858	MILP that are polynomially solvable. In particular, they showed that the inverse problem can be solved in
   859	polynomial time whenever the forward problem is polynomially solvable.
   860	Theorem 2. (Ahuja and Orlin [2001]) If an optimization problem is polynomially solvable for each linear
   861	cost function, then the corresponding inverse problems under the `1 and `∞ norms are polynomially solvable.
   862	
   863	Ahuja and Orlin [2001] use Theorem 1 from Grötschel et al. [1993] to conclude that inverse LP, in particular,
   864	is polynomially solvable. The separation problem in this case is an LP of polynomial input size and is hence
   865	polynomially solvable. The theorem of Grötschel et al. [1993] shows that both problems are in the class P,
   866	since Karp and Cook reductions are equivalent for problems in P. Theorem 2 also indicates that if a given
   867	MILP is polynomially solvable, then the associated inverse problem is also polynomially solvable.
